can you find the seal on the ball  it's hiding behind those blocks  what about the rubber band  that's over by the alligator  does this look familiar  these photos are from the i spy book  series introduced in 1992 and there's a  good chance that either you or your kids  have read them but did you know that all  of these images were created with real  objects the magic and mystery within  these books is directly tied to the  imagination of the photographer behind  them walter wick  my name is walter wick i am a photo  illustrator and i've been doing that for  i don't know how long since 1991. before  i spy walter was a commercial  photographer in new york city one of his  clients was games magazine which created  puzzles and activities for each issue  but how did walter end up working on i  spy one day around 1980 walter was  cleaning up his studio and he decided to  take a picture of the stray nuts and  bolts he had lying around walter used  that photo to promote his work which is  how the author jean marzolo saw it  i got a call from g marzolo to do a  poster for  her magazine which is called let's find  out it's a kindergarten magazine an  editor at scholastic books saw that  poster and asked if gene and walter  wanted to make a search and find  children's book gene would write the  rhymes and walter would create the  images and i said sure and that's how it  started  but perhaps what made the book so  successful was how realistic they looked  the combination of props sets and  lighting created an immersive scene  originally walter used a large format  camera to photograph each scene this  created huge 8x10 negatives which was  ideal because the larger image size  captured a ton of detail  then around 2004 walter switched to  digital he now uses a canon 5d but  walter was quick to point out that the  camera does little of the real work  because it just sits there while walter  spends weeks building the sets walter's  studio is filled with the props and  materials he uses to build these  incredible images like blocks buttons  toys and even pine cones  i would hide the objects as i would  build the shot i wouldn't really think  about building the hiding the objects  first i would create the shot make a  list of the hidden objects send it to  jean and then she would write the rhyme  each scene is arranged very carefully  around a central idea or theme like  nature antiques or school  one of the problems with photo  illustrations is you're limited by  what you have available the higher level  concepts became harder and harder and  harder to do though as i went on because  i didn't want to repeat myself  but i spy isn't the only series walter  has worked on he's also the author and  photo illustrator of the can you see  what i see books and hey seymour which  follow the search and find style of i  spy in order to make these scenes a  reality walter works with assistants  fabricators and model makers for can you  see what i see toyland express walter's  team built a toy train from scratch  because they needed to show it being  assembled throughout the story each  photo can take anywhere from a couple  days to several weeks to create the  attention to detail is part of what  makes these photos so compelling and  walter continues to capture the  imagination of readers today his newest  book array of light is a science book  that uses beautiful photos to illustrate  the properties of light it's a companion  to walter's 1997 book a drop of water  i've always wanted to do the book online  i was a little afraid of it because as  much as i work with light and i  know light i was a little afraid to  trying to explain the inner workings of  it to young readers  but i finally did that  the i spy and can you see what ic books  have over 45 million copies in print the  care and attention to detail put into  every frame still resonates with readers  today i was very surprised right from  the very beginning  how intensely kids  looked at my photographs  so i just put this same intensity back  into making it  imagination and the sense of discovery  are timeless  [Music]
 

